<Layout
  path="/roadmap"
  title="Roadmap - Nuthatch"
  description="What is shipped, in progress, and planned in Nuthatch, generated from the repo README's working-now table, the release notes, and the numbered RFCs. Dates are not promised; order is."
>
  <section class="page">
    <article class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="section-label">Roadmap</p>
      <h1>Roadmap</h1>

      <p>
        Nuthatch plans in public. The work is numbered as RFCs in the repository, every
        release has its own notes, and the load-bearing claims come with a
        measured number rather than a promise. This page is the map over that ground.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reading key is plain. <strong>Shipped</strong> means merged with tests and, where
        it matters, a measured number. <strong>In progress</strong> means partially landed,
        with the remainder tracked. <strong>Planned</strong> means an accepted next step - an
        RFC in the repository, or one still to be written (marked "RFC to come").
        <strong>Not planned</strong> means exactly that. Dates are not promised - order is.
      </p>

      <!-- SHIPPED -->
      <h2 class="stat s-shipped">Shipped</h2>
      <p class="grp">The embedded single-binary pipeline, end to end</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>init</code> resolves each contract ABI (Sourcify, then an Etherscan-class API, with EIP-1967 and legacy-OZ proxy resolution) and scaffolds the nest: a <code>schema.json</code>, a <code>semantic.toml</code> seeded from the ABI, a commented <code>views/</code> starter, an <code>llms.txt</code>, and a Claude Code skill.</li>
        <li>Deterministic decode of every declared event of every contract - a topic0-keyed registry, one table per <code>{'{'}alias{'}'}__{'{'}event{'}'}</code>.</li>
        <li>Every row carries provenance and ergonomics columns: <code>block_hash</code>, a deterministic <code>_seq</code>, <code>block_timestamp</code>, and <code>*_dec</code>/<code>*_overflow</code> siblings so big integers sum without hand-casting.</li>
        <li>Reorg self-healing: block-hash checkpoints roll the hot store back; a proptest asserts convergence with the winning branch.</li>
        <li>Finality-gated, content-addressed Parquet sealing, with the hot store pruned once every table's segment is durable.</li>
        <li>Read-only analytical SQL over one surface (Burrmill, on DataFusion, since 4.1.0): <code>/sql</code> unions the sealed segments with the live hot tip, and <code>GET /tables</code> / <code>GET /table/{'{'}name{'}'}</code> merge hot and cold - the full data model, queryable to the head.</li>
        <li>Balance view maintained incrementally (DBSP): i128 base units serialised as decimal strings, a reorg is a retraction, and it replays from stored facts on restart.</li>
        <li>Serving over HTTP and MCP: the MCP server speaks stdio, fully offline (see the agent-grade MCP surface below).</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">The delightful core (<a href={rfc('0015')} rel="noopener">RFC-0015</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nuthatch sql</code> - one-shot SQL from the terminal (aligned table, <code>--json</code> to pipe), plus an interactive REPL with <code>.tables</code>/<code>.schema</code>, history and line editing. Queries the local store when <code>dev</code> is stopped, and falls back to the running instance when it holds the writer.</li>
        <li>Magical <code>init</code> - omit <code>--chain</code> and nuthatch probes each supported chain for your contract's bytecode and picks the one it lives on.</li>
        <li><code>nuthatch add</code> - grow a nest with another contract, no re-init; existing contracts resume from their cursor while the new one backfills from its deployment.</li>
        <li>Live backfill feedback - a progress line while it catches up, and a clear "caught up to tip" the moment it does.</li>
        <li>Production recipes - <code>nuthatch mcp --print-config</code> prints a copy-paste MCP config, and systemd / docker unit recipes run <code>dev</code> unattended.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Governed semantic layer &amp; agent-grade MCP (<a href={rfc('0016')} rel="noopener">RFC-0016</a>, <a href={rfc('0017')} rel="noopener">RFC-0017</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A per-nest semantic layer (<code>semantic.toml</code>, seeded from the ABI at <code>init</code>): the <code>schema</code> tool serves <em>this</em> nest's meaning - table/column descriptions, the hot/cold coverage seam, and derived footguns (reserved-word and big-int columns) - not one static string for every nest.</li>
        <li>Errors as prompts - a failed query returns a one-line fix hint (nearest real table/column, reserved word, big-int) over the raw engine message; <code>explain</code> validates a query without executing it, so an agent self-corrects in one round-trip.</li>
        <li>Result shaping + provenance - MCP results come back as compact aligned tables with truncation-as-guidance and a citable stamp (<code>as of block N, sealed_through M, registry &lt;hash&gt;</code>).</li>
        <li>The full MCP surface - 12 tools (eight on every nest, <code>diff</code> among them, two more on a token nest, two with compliance), 3 resources (<code>nuthatch://schema|tables|status</code>), and 3 prompts (<code>profile-contract</code>, <code>investigate-address</code>, <code>verify-a-number</code>), all offline; nothing in the data path touched.</li>
        <li>A repo-installable Claude Code skill (<a href={rfc('0017')} rel="noopener">RFC-0017</a>) teaches an agent to <em>drive</em> nuthatch - init, config, factories, runtimes, ops - with CLI/config references generated from the source and CI-checked for drift.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Nests</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>init --from &lt;git-url | ./dir&gt;</code> - a nest is a git repo, so it needs no registry to share; the vendored ABIs and deployment blocks travel with it. A content-addressed <a href={rfc('0019')} rel="noopener">registry</a> is the optional package path when you want <code>name@version</code> and private nests.</li>
        <li>A schema-version guard rejects a nest declaring a newer format, which is what makes consuming a third party's nest safe.</li>
        <li>Authored <code>views/*.sql</code> (<a href={rfc('0018')} rel="noopener">RFC-0018</a>) are a validated, drift-gated layer: each view binds against the schema at load - so a column that moves out from under it is a loud <code>nuthatch check</code> failure, not a silent wrong number - and its meaning is described in <code>semantic.toml</code> and surfaced through <code>/schema</code>.</li>
        <li>Authored incremental entities (<a href={rfc('0041')} rel="noopener">RFC-0041</a>) let a nest declare a bounded keyed relation in <code>entities.toml</code>. It is maintained as blocks arrive, retracts on reorg, is queryable through <code>/sql</code> and <code>/derived</code>, and carries its own watermark and Prometheus signals. General <code>views/*.sql</code> remain query-time SQL; the distinction is explicit rather than aspirational.</li>
        <li><code>nuthatch check</code> runs a nest's <code>checks/*.sql</code> against recorded fixtures - the generic parity and invariant framework, hermetic in CI.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Chains</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>Built in: Ethereum mainnet, Arbitrum One, Base, BSC, Polygon, Gnosis, Optimism, Monad and Robinhood Chain, with measured public endpoints and tuned finality settings; the L2s read their L1-aware <code>finalized</code> tag with a depth fallback, so a new chain is a registry entry, not a fork of the loop.</li>
        <li>Any other EVM chain works by naming it and passing <code>--rpc</code>; the chain id is read from the endpoint itself.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Backfill throughput, measured</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nuthatch bench backfill</code> reports the median of events per second, wall-clock, peak RSS and RPC requests; every published number traces to a report artifact.</li>
        <li>Hot-store, seal-direct and pipelined paths produce byte-identical segments. The former ≈8.7× seal-direct and ≈20× stacked multipliers were withdrawn in v2.7.0: their denominator came from a pre-fix harness which did not use the indexer's real commit path, and public-RPC remeasurement produced contradictory results. A deterministic replay rig comes before another multiplier.</li>
        <li>An adaptive <code>getLogs</code> chunker targets ≈2,000 logs per response and self-heals into any provider's result cap, instead of a hand-tuned constant.</li>
        <li><code>dev --seal-direct</code> writes finalised history straight to Parquet, rebuilds the balance view, then hands off to the tip loop. Since 2.7.0 it resolves declared <code>[[calls]]</code> on every direct-seal path rather than refusing the combination.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Operator surface</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A Prometheus <code>GET /metrics</code> endpoint: tip height, tip lag, sealed-through, decode / seal / reorg / HTTP / SQL counters, and process RSS.</li>
        <li>A <code>GET /ready</code> readiness endpoint (200 fresh, 503 when the tip poll has stalled) and an escalating log when RPC stops answering.</li>
        <li>Health-aware RPC failover - a failing endpoint is benched on a cooldown so calls route to healthy ones first - and startup segment recovery that hash-verifies each sealed segment and quarantines any that is corrupt or tampered.</li>
        <li><code>/sql</code> query guards - a wall-clock timeout, a row cap, a result-byte ceiling, a concurrency limit, a filesystem-access denylist, and single-statement enforcement (a <code>;</code>-stacked statement is refused before anything runs).</li>
        <li>Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM (drains, checkpoints, resumes without gaps) and a loud warning when bound off localhost.</li>
        <li>A published stability contract, and a <code>cargo-deny</code> CI gate over advisories, licences, and sources.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Factories and dynamic discovery (<a href={rfc('0009')} rel="noopener">RFC-0009</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>[[templates]]</code> and <code>[[factories]]</code> index the child contracts a factory deploys at runtime - tip, backfill, and reorg-safe, with a <code>{'{'}template{'}'}__children</code> view. It indexes Uniswap.</li>
        <li>The discovered set flips from an address list to a topic0 filter at scale, so a factory with thousands of children stays a single deterministic getLogs stream.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Compliance pack (<a href={rfc('0008')} rel="noopener">RFC-0008</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>Threshold and velocity flags (per-transfer annotations plus a DBSP windowed velocity view, i128, reorg = retraction), served at <code>/flags</code>.</li>
        <li>Direct counterparty-exposure view (DBSP) over content-addressed label snapshots, served at <code>/exposure/{'{'}addr{'}'}</code>.</li>
        <li>Alert webhooks POST flag annotations at-least-once through a durable outbox that never blocks the indexer.</li>
        <li>Signed compliance-pack manifest (<code>pack build</code>/<code>verify</code>, ed25519), and <code>audit report</code> summarises the threshold flags over a range. Live sanctions screening, its WASM component and <code>audit replay</code> were removed in 5.0.0.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Admin UI and webhooks (<a href={rfc('0010')} rel="noopener">RFC-0010</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A built-in admin UI at <code>/_admin/</code> - embedded, no framework or CDN: status, table browser, SQL runner, and nest inspector, read-only and localhost-gated.</li>
        <li>User webhooks: <code>[[webhooks]]</code> POST sealed rows matching a <code>where</code> clause to a URL through the shared at-least-once outbox; <code>since</code> suppresses backfill history.</li>
        <li>Signed webhook egress: a webhook <code>secret</code> signs each delivery with <code>X-Nuthatch-Signature: sha256=&lt;hmac&gt;</code> over the exact bytes sent.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Nest packaging (<a href={rfc('0012')} rel="noopener">RFC-0012</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nuthatch nest bundle</code> packs a nest's authored inputs into a single portable, content-addressed <code>.bundle</code> file whose hash is a Merkle root over a canonical manifest; <code>nest load</code> verifies and installs one - from a local file or an <code>http(s)</code> URL - regenerating the decode registry from the installed inputs and asserting it matches the author's pinned hash. Determinism carried across the wire, not just the disk.</li>
        <li>One runtime hosts several nests on the same chain under a single cursor, one hot store, and one finality view, with strict per-nest isolation and a per-cursor RSS budget: a mount whose projection exceeds the budget is refused before it starts. Verified live on Arbitrum. <em>(2.0 retired the separate <code>roost</code> command - it is <code>nuthatch dev</code>, and the directory decides what runs.)</em></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Nest registry (<a href={rfc('0019')} rel="noopener">RFC-0019</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nest publish &lt;bundle&gt; --registry &lt;path|s3://…&gt; --as name@version</code> writes a bundle to a content-addressed store; <code>nest load name@version --registry …</code> resolves, fetches, and installs it - the pulled blob hash-verified by the same install path, so a registry pull is as safe as a hash-pinned file load. A filesystem path <em>is</em> a registry; an S3-compatible bucket (MinIO/S3/R2) is the fleet path, behind <code>--features object-store</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Private nests</strong> live behind your bucket's own auth (<code>AWS_*</code> env, including <code>AWS_ENDPOINT</code>); a missing or rejected credential fails with a clear "this nest is private", never a bare "not found". Self-hosted-first: the registry is decoupled and never mandatory - a self-built bundle and <code>load &lt;file|dir&gt;</code> touch no registry at all.</li>
        <li><strong>A bundle is also addressed by its NID</strong> (3.13.0): <code>nest publish</code> records it under the identity <code>nest nid</code> prints, and <code>nest load &lt;nid&gt; --registry …</code> refuses a bundle whose recomputed NID differs from the one asked for.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Safe upgrades - no resync tax (<a href={rfc('0020')} rel="noopener">RFC-0020</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A nest update is classified at the moment its identity changes: <em>compatible</em> (additive only - a new table or column) or <em>breaking</em> (a consumer-observable change - removed, renamed, or retyped). <code>nuthatch migrate</code> applies a compatible update, and an edit that leaves what is stored unchanged adopts the existing dataset with no re-index; a breaking update is named and refused until <code>--allow-breaking</code>. On a running runtime, <code>POST /_admin/move/&lt;name&gt;</code> catches a new version up beside the old and switches the name's routes in one step, so readers never see an error between. Either way, updating a nest stops being a subgraph-style genesis resync. <code>nest diff</code> and <code>nest upgrade</code> were the 0.6 shape and were removed in 2.0; see <a href="/docs/operate/upgrades/">Upgrading a nest</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Multichain roost (<a href={rfc('0021')} rel="noopener">RFC-0021</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A runtime can host nests across <strong>more than one chain</strong> in a single runtime - <code>[[chains]]</code> in <code>mounts.toml</code>, and one <strong>isolated cursor per chain</strong> (its own tip, finality, and reorg boundary): a Base nest and an Arbitrum nest served from one process. The footprint budget is <strong>per active-chain cursor</strong> (≤2 GB), and each cursor is its own observable boundary, held per chain. A capability, not a mandate: one chain per runtime stays the simple default and is byte-identical to running the nest solo. The single-cursor law holds per chain - two chains are never multiplexed behind one cursor.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Derive-first - the eth_call you don't need (<a href={rfc('0023')} rel="noopener">RFC-0023</a>, tiers 1-3 shipped; row-driven calls in <a href={rfc('0038')} rel="noopener">RFC-0038</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>RFC-0023 cites The Graph Foundation for the figure that over 70% of subgraphs call <code>eth_call</code> - but most of those reads are <em>derivable</em> from the events a nest already indexes; subgraphs fetch them only because they have no incremental-view engine. Nuthatch does. <code>nuthatch recipe add total_supply</code> drops in a derived view that computes an ERC-20's <code>totalSupply()</code> as &#931; minted &#8722; &#931; burned from Transfer events - deterministic, free, and with no archive node. It derives what a subgraph pays an archive node to fetch. The ERC-20-generic trio - <code>total_supply</code>, <code>balances</code>, and <code>holder_count</code> - all derive from Transfer events, and <code>reserves</code> derives Uniswap-V2 <code>getReserves()</code> as the latest <code>Sync</code> per pair - the AMM read a subgraph fetches on every swap. For the handful of reads that aren't derivable but never change - <code>decimals</code>/<code>symbol</code>/<code>name</code> - <code>nuthatch metadata fetch</code> calls once and caches forever. For the genuinely non-derivable residue, a verifiable eth_call fallback follows; its local-execution engine (a demand-driven state cache, not an archive node) is designed but deferred, so the derive-first path stays fully self-hosted with no external data dependency.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Fault quarantine - a runtime survives its sick nests (<a href={rfc('0026')} rel="noopener">RFC-0026</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A faulting nest is <strong>quarantined</strong> rather than fatal: its healthy siblings keep indexing and serving, and it is re-admitted on a backoff if the fault was retryable. A terminal fault stays quarantined until an operator fixes it. The blast radius is now bounded in both directions - a nest's error no longer kills its cursor, and a cursor's death no longer kills the runtime.</li>
        <li>The health surface to see it with: <code>GET /nests</code> merges each nest's live health per request, <code>GET /ready</code> answers runtime-wide while <code>GET /&lt;name&gt;/ready</code> answers per nest, and <code>nuthatch_nest_health</code> / <code>nuthatch_nest_quarantine_total</code> / <code>nuthatch_cursor_live</code> cover the metrics. <code>--fail-fast</code> restores exit-on-first-fault for CI and for operators who prefer a loud death to partial service.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">The live roost (<a href={rfc('0027')} rel="noopener">RFC-0027</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A runtime's nest set used to be frozen at boot, so onboarding one tenant's nest restarted every <em>co-tenant's</em> - the blast radius of a config change was larger than that of a fault. Now <code>POST /_admin/nests</code> mounts one and <code>DELETE /_admin/nests/&lt;name&gt;</code> unmounts one, live. A mount is admitted only if it fits the cursor's RAM budget (refused with <code>507</code>, never a warning - a budget that can be quietly exceeded is not a budget), catches up <em>before</em> it joins so it never drags co-tenants back through history, and only then gets routes. An unmount is a <strong>drain</strong>: the cursor finishes its window and releases the store before anything is torn down.</li>
        <li><strong>The runtime runs its own nests</strong> (3.12.2 and 3.13.0). A runtime may start with its chains declared and nothing mounted; the first mount onto a chain starts that chain's cursor. Started with <code>--registry</code>, it fetches a NID it does not hold, verifies it, and removes it again if the mount is then refused. A mount is a job, answered <code>202</code> and readable at <code>GET /_admin/mounts/&lt;name&gt;</code> without waiting on the mount; jobs survive a restart. A mount can be dry-run for its backfill, per-block RPC work and footprint, suspended behind a <code>503</code> and resumed from where it stopped, moved to a new NID with no gap a reader can see, and reclaimed once nothing mounts it. <code>/metrics</code> reports storage per nest. <a href="/docs/operate/hosting-nests">Host nests for others</a> walks through it.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Scaffold a nest from a subgraph (<a href={rfc('0019')} rel="noopener">RFC-0019</a>, <a href={REPO + '/issues/241'} rel="noopener">#241</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nuthatch init --from-subgraph &lt;CID&gt;</code> maps a subgraph manifest's <code>dataSources</code> to <code>[[contracts]]</code> and its <code>templates</code> to <code>[[templates]]</code>, vendors every ABI from the CID the manifest pins, and carries <code>startBlock</code> and each dataSource's <code>eventHandlers</code> across - so a subgraph that handled only <code>Transfer</code> does not silently grow a table for every other event. The pinned ABIs beat Sourcify on proxy-heavy codebases, where Sourcify returns the <em>proxy</em> ABI that declares none of the events the contract emits. Factory rules are inferred only where a parameter unambiguously names a template; anything ambiguous is reported with candidates rather than guessed, because a wrong guess is a nest that quietly indexes the wrong children. <strong>Our first external contribution</strong>, from <a href="https://github.com/PaulieB14" rel="noopener">@PaulieB14</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">The backfill that finishes, then flies (<a href={rfc('0028')} rel="noopener">RFC-0028</a>, <a href={rfc('0029')} rel="noopener">RFC-0029</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>Found by running <strong>someone else's benchmark</strong>: Sentio's <a href="https://github.com/sentioxyz/open-blockchain-indexer-benchmark" rel="noopener">OBIB</a>. Case 1 - <code>Transfer</code> from LBTC across 22.2M Ethereum blocks - did not merely run slowly, it <strong>never finished</strong>: Alchemy returns its oversized-range refusal as HTTP <strong>400</strong>, a status the classifier did not enumerate, so a window that needed splitting was retried unchanged forever. It now completes, with the record count Sentio publishes (294,278 events). That run used an Alchemy account that has since closed, so its timings are not reproducible and are not quoted here; the case that is, OBIB case 6 on the published 4.7.0 binary, is on the home page.</li>
        <li>The rest was round-trip economics rather than compute: most of the wall clock was buying <code>block_timestamp</code>, one serial fan-out inside every window, for a column that workload never stores. Timestamps are now <strong>demand-driven</strong> - a nest that does not ask does not pay - and the log window <strong>adapts</strong>, widening while an endpoint keeps up and narrowing the instant it does not, so a provider's undocumented limits are discovered rather than configured.</li>
        <li>RPC failures are also classified properly now: an auth rejection stops being retried forever, an unclassifiable wide-range failure splits speculatively rather than stalling, and a 429 escalates pool-wide.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Scaled mode - a fleet (<a href={rfc('0022')} rel="noopener">RFC-0022</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>One operator across many machines: a control plane holding <em>desired state</em>, a writer pool taking <strong>one lease per chain cursor</strong> with the fence enforced inside the store's own write transaction, and a query-FE tier scaled independently of ingestion. A plan is not permission - a scheduler that wants to rehome a live cursor is refused by the lease, which is correct rather than a bug.</li>
        <li>Workers <strong>pull the nests they are assigned</strong> from a registry (<a href={rfc('0019')} rel="noopener">RFC-0019</a>), because the machine the scheduler picks may have nothing on disk. With a <code>bundle_hash</code> pinned the fetch is by <strong>content address</strong> and the registry's mutable index is never consulted, so re-tagging a version cannot change what a fleet runs.</li>
        <li>Verified across <strong>real machines</strong>, not several processes on one box: a genuine lease handover with the fence advancing, a 10-minute clock jump moving expiry rather than ownership, and <strong>377 blocks indexed through a 90-second control-plane outage</strong> - losing the control plane stops rescheduling, not ingestion.</li>
        <li><strong>And it did not write for a whole release.</strong> Until 0.9.3 <code>worker</code> registered, took leases, loaded secrets and reported - and contained no indexing code at all. Ten level-5 checks passed throughout, because every one of them tested the control plane and none asserted that a row appeared. We would rather you read that here than discover it.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Maintained views (<a href={rfc('0062')} rel="noopener">RFC-0062</a>, 4.14.0)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A view listed in <code>maintained.toml</code> is answered from a stored copy of its own request-time evaluation, keyed on every input it read: the binary, the authored files, the segments and the hot rows. A request uses the copy only when its inputs hash the same, so a copy cannot be stale or differ. A nest that declares none behaves as before.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">The head count (<a href={rfc('0054')} rel="noopener">RFC-0054</a>, 4.15.0)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>After a successful <code>init</code>, nuthatch asks once whether it may count you. Enter is no. A yes sends two kinds of event with no identifier, and the tally is <a href="/count">public</a>. It is the one network call the binary makes about an operator, and it is off until a person turns it on.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Address history (<a href={rfc('0063')} rel="noopener">RFC-0063</a>, <a href={rfc('0064')} rel="noopener">RFC-0064</a>, 5.1.0 to 5.7.0)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A nest that watches accounts rather than contracts, and serves their history at <code>/api</code> in Etherscan's shapes, so <a href="/rotki">rotki</a> can use it as an indexer. Answers are exact or refused. It covers Ethereum, Optimism, Base, Arbitrum, Polygon, BNB Chain and Gnosis, several chains behind one endpoint, and proven block proposers for validators on mainnet.</li>
        <li><code>nuthatch partitions</code> builds verified block partitions a nest can start from instead of an RPC backfill; <a href="/nests/hosted/ethereum-address-history">the Ethereum ones are published</a>. The deposit sweep (5.4.0) reads each block once for a whole watched set, so its cost is per block rather than per address.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">The diff (<a href={rfc('0071')} rel="noopener">RFC-0071</a>, 5.9.0)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><code>nuthatch diff</code>, <code>GET /diff</code> and the MCP <code>diff</code> tool tell what happened in a block range, a block or a transaction in fixed sentences with exact amounts, from the rows a nest already holds. No model, and nothing inferred.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">A nest in a browser tab (<a href={rfc('0065')} rel="noopener">RFC-0065</a>, <a href={rfc('0066')} rel="noopener">RFC-0066</a>, <a href={rfc('0068')} rel="noopener">RFC-0068</a>)</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><a href="/play">The playground</a> runs decode, sealing and Burrmill compiled to WebAssembly in a tab, queries a published nest's segments checked by hash, and keeps it current from public RPCs. <a href="/play/build">The nest builder</a> scaffolds a nest from a contract address with <code>init</code>'s own code and indexes it in the tab. A postcard is a <code>/play</code> link that pins a published nest's segments by hash. None of it changes the binary.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="grp">Release and footprint</p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li><strong>v5.9.0 is the current release</strong>, and 5.x is the stable line. It adds <code>nuthatch diff</code>, which tells what happened in a block range, a block or a transaction in fixed sentences from the rows a nest already holds, with no model and no storage migration (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v5.9.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>5.8.2</strong> keeps a seal-direct backfill on a dense chain inside the per-cursor memory budget: a blocks nest at 3,000 transactions per block peaked at 3,090 MiB and now peaks at 245 MiB (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v5.8.2" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>5.8.1</strong> refuses to reopen a store, or reuse its segments, after an event's indexed parameters have moved (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v5.8.1" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>5.8.0</strong> was a cold audit of 5.7.2 put right, 107 verified defects across stored-state correctness, isolation between tenants and what an untrusted caller can reach; several defaults are stricter, among them the admin token, which now applies on localhost too (<a href="/docs/operate/upgrades/">upgrade notes</a>, <a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v5.8.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>5.7.2</strong> moved <code>init</code>'s output and the nest identity into crates a browser tab can build, for the <a href="/play/build">nest builder</a>. Address-history nests serve <a href="/rotki">rotki</a> on Ethereum and six more chains, several of them from one address, with verified Ethereum validator proposals from block 23,551,570, withdrawals, produced blocks and MEV. Requests have an eight-second default deadline, and incomplete history is refused. <strong>5.0.1</strong> shipped 5.0.0, which was tagged but never published: 4.15.2 with the WASM transform layer and live screening removed (<code>nuthatch transform</code>, <code>nuthatch screen</code>, <code>nuthatch audit replay</code>, <code>[screening]</code>, the <code>screen_status</code> MCP tool, and wasmtime with them); a nest with a <code>[screening]</code> table loads once the table is deleted. The <code>exex</code> stub is gone too. Release binaries are built with fat LTO, about a third smaller on macOS, and Arrow and Parquet 59 replace the second copy of 58, so a segment sealed by 5.0.0 hashes differently from the same rows sealed by 4.15; the decoded rows are identical and nothing is re-indexed (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v5.0.1" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>4.15.2</strong>: a nest without <code>--poll-interval</code> polls every 5 minutes. <strong>4.15.1</strong> fixed a GraphQL <code>Bytes</code> filter given a checksummed address. <strong>4.15.0</strong> added the opt-in <a href="/count">head count</a>, <strong>4.14.0</strong> maintained views, and <strong>4.13.0</strong> narrowed pages over left joins. <strong>4.12.0</strong> (2026-10-06): a cheaper engine on view-heavy nests (shared subqueries planned once, compacted join build sides; <a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/pull/1965" rel="noopener">#1965</a>), prebuilt Linux arm64 binaries for the default and graph builds (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/pull/1963" rel="noopener">#1963</a>), and a shutdown that no longer waits on an open admin tab (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/pull/1967" rel="noopener">#1967</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>4.11.0</strong> (2026-10-06) attaches a <code>nuthatch-graph-&lt;target&gt;</code> download to every release, the only build that serves Graph-dialect GraphQL; the default binary still has no GraphQL route. That GraphQL now answers more client shapes (a to-one relation below another, a single <code>@derivedFrom</code> field, a stored scalar list) and refuses an unknown field or an unserved collection by name. A factory nest asks for logs by factory and child addresses until 500 children, an endpoint that refuses too many addresses in one <code>eth_getLogs</code> has the list halved rather than being read as a credentials refusal, and <code>port-emit</code> with no mapping source exits non-zero instead of reporting full coverage. The SQL answer cache keys on the rows and segments a statement reads, so a quiet nest at the tip answers a repeated statement from the cache; a cold-only answer reports its source as <code>sealed</code>; <code>nuthatch_publish_lag_blocks</code> counts final segments the mirror lacks; and a scaled-mode worker that loses a lease stops ingesting that cursor on the same tick (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v4.11.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>4.10.1</strong> (2026-10-05): four fixes, among them a zero block timestamp from a public Arbitrum endpoint, which now refuses the window instead of being stored (#1911), and an authored entity that no longer claims a block before the store has committed it. <strong>4.10.0</strong> makes the engine count the memory a scan holds and adds the budget settings <code>NUTHATCH_RUNTIME_HEADROOM</code> and a lower <code>NUTHATCH_INGESTION_RESERVATION</code>; on a copy of the QoS nest, two statements at a time, peak RSS went from 1,746 to 1,749 MiB down to 1,298 to 1,349 MiB (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v4.10.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>4.9.0</strong> added <code>nuthatch seed</code>, which starts a nest from a published mirror instead of an RPC backfill (<a href="/mirror">the public mirror</a>): a clean clone of graph-gns-nest took 2.4 s for 23 segments and 18.9 MB (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v4.9.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). <strong>4.8.0</strong> made a first run reach its data in seconds, 70 to 72 s before and 10 to 11 s after for the quickstart in a clean <code>ubuntu:24.04</code> container, and added <code>publish finalise</code> for an archive nest's last segments. <strong>4.7.0</strong> undid a slowdown 4.6.0 brought to joins on computed keys, routed around a throttled endpoint instead of retrying one range forever, and stopped derived views counting a re-read sealed block twice. <strong>4.6.0</strong> made float <code>SUM</code> and <code>AVG</code> reproducible and added the opt-in <code>[extract] l1_blocks</code> table for Arbitrum. <strong>4.5.0</strong> added a sampled audit of sealed history against a second endpoint (<code>--audit-rpc</code>, <code>nuthatch audit sealed</code>) and the <code>nuthatch_entity_state_bytes</code> gauge. <strong>4.4.1</strong> fixed wrong answers in 4.2.1 through 4.4.0, where an integer division over a HUGEINT that had passed through <code>COALESCE</code> or <code>IFNULL</code> was computed in floating point. <strong>4.4.0</strong>: every analytics session over one nest shares one memory pool, so the limit bounds the nest rather than each statement, and a range join over a small table skips Parquet row groups it cannot match; on a copy of the QoS nest, kittiwake's one-day statements went from 7.2 to 11.9 s down to 1.1 to 1.3 s (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v4.4.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>). 
<strong>4.3.1</strong> fixed a wrong answer in 4.3.0, where a join under a memory budget could drop matches. <strong>4.3.0</strong> lowered Burrmill's peak memory under a budget (on the Lodestar allocations nest's query set, from 3.3 GiB to under 1.9 GiB). <strong>4.2.1</strong> rebuilt the admin page at <code>/_admin/</code> with eight tabs. <strong>4.2.0</strong> fixed a memory regression in 4.1.1 and brought more of Burrmill's answers in line with DuckDB's. <strong>4.1.1</strong> closed the findings of a cold audit of 4.1.0, among them a <code>/sql</code> statement that could stop the process. <strong>4.1.0</strong> changed the SQL engine (below), removed <code>emit dune</code>, and raised the Linux glibc floor to 2.35. <strong>4.0.2</strong> was 4.0.1 with a result cap under the rate-limit code narrowed instead of retried. 4.0.1 is 4.0.0 with mainnet default endpoints that serve history. 4.0.0 is the stable line: 3.13.3 plus three small fixes, and a promise that config, data directories and the HTTP, SQL and MCP surfaces keep working through every later 4.x (<a href="/docs/operate/upgrades/#the-5x-promise">the promise, now carried by 5.x</a>). A backfill whose RPC endpoints have pruned its history says so and names the remedy, <code>nuthatch sql</code> and the MCP SQL tool print columns in the query's order and a <code>/sql</code> response gains a <code>columns</code> array, and the first-run demo starts near the tip with <code>nuthatch dev --backfill 300</code>. <strong>3.13.3</strong> made <code>nuthatch check</code> lower and bind each entity as <code>dev</code> starts it, so the two agree, made <code>port-emit</code> entities start, had <code>nest bundle</code> refuse credentials, and made a restart, an idle entity fault and a finality violation all report truthfully. <strong>3.13.2</strong> was a hardening pass over what 3.13.1 does. <strong>3.13.1</strong> fixed 3.13.0's runtime admin API, found by running it end to end against a real chain. <strong>3.9.0</strong> added <code>nuthatch nest nid</code>, which prints the identity a nest's data is stored under without running it, and <code>nuthatch offchain pull</code>, a one-shot fetch that seals a JSON feed as an offchain snapshot (<a href={rfc('0045')} rel="noopener">RFC-0045</a> stage 2); authored entities can now read those snapshots. An <code>eth_call</code> error that is not an explicit revert is no longer stored as a revert: the nest retries and then stalls at that block, so a nest calling through a pruned endpoint needs an archive one. An entity whose SQL is inline now refuses to start and names the file to create. <strong>3.10.0</strong> moved the GraphQL routes out of the default build: the published binary and images answer them with 404, and a deployment serving GraphQL clients builds with <code>--features graph</code>. A second live mount of a dataset that is already mounted now shares its store. <strong>3.11.0</strong> is about the RPC bill: a backfill takes <code>block_timestamp</code> from the logs rather than buying a header per block (4,444 header reads down to 34 on the same 20,000-block Sepolia backfill, with byte-identical segments), idle polls skip the reorg check, the default poll interval is the chain's block time (12 s on Ethereum, never below 2 s), and <code>--rpc-fallback</code> names endpoints asked only while every other one is failing. <strong>3.12.0</strong> changes nothing in the published binaries; its work is behind the off-by-default <code>folds</code> feature. <strong>3.12.1</strong> executes each <code>/sql</code> statement once rather than twice, which every query had paid since 3.8.5, and answers a query cut off by the time budget with 504 rather than 400. <strong>3.12.2</strong> moves Wasmtime past two security advisories, holds a nest mounted on a running runtime to the rules boot enforces, and lets a runtime start with nothing mounted. <strong>3.13.0</strong> lets a runtime run its own nests' lifecycle over the admin API, reports storage per nest, and stops the runtime-wide sealed total counting a shared segment store once per nest; a mount now answers <code>202</code>, and <code>?wait=true</code> keeps the old answer. Each is a binary swap and a restart, apart from the 3.9.0 refusals and the 3.10.0 GraphQL routes.</li>
        <li><strong>Burrmill is the analytical engine from 4.1.0</strong> (2026-10-02), a Rust query engine on DataFusion, and DuckDB is no longer in the binary (<a href="/blog/replacing-duckdb-after-all">the decision</a>, which reverses <a href="/blog/keeping-duckdb">Keeping DuckDB</a>). It was not chosen for speed: measured on a production nest on 2026-10-01, it takes about 2.5&times; DuckDB's time per statement and needs more memory for the same joins. What it buys is one language in the binary and exact arithmetic that refuses rather than wraps. Before the cutover it ran in a shadow session on <code>main</code>, answering beside DuckDB while DuckDB's answer was served (<a href="/blog/switching-the-engine-under-a-live-indexer">the log of the switch</a>). External readers such as DuckDB or Trino still read the sealed Parquet.</li>
        <li><strong>3.8.0</strong> makes an <code>[[ipfs]]</code> nest over megabyte documents fit to deploy (<a href={rfc('0037')} rel="noopener">RFC-0037</a> slices 5 to 8). A CID can be read out of JSON in an event or a call with <code>cid_json_path</code> and <code>json_match</code>; every document resolves behind the cursor with retry and a recorded give-up, and sealing holds below any block whose documents are still undecided; a multi-block document is proven against its CID, and an unproven one writes no row; <code>[ipfs.rows]</code> explodes a proven JSON document into typed rows. A seal cut is now also bounded at 64 MiB of row JSON: the Edge &amp; Node QoS nest on Gnosis had sealed 1,342 megabyte documents at 11.35 GB of RSS. Alongside: <code>--cors</code> on <code>dev</code> and <code>serve</code>, <code>--explorer</code> for a Blockscout instance on <code>init</code> and <code>add</code>, <code>init --from-subgraph</code> over a local <code>subgraph.yaml</code>, and a <code>doctor</code> that finds its own probe address. An events-only nest swaps the binary and restarts; a nest that has indexed top-level calls re-indexes, and one with <code>[[calls]]</code>, <code>[[ipfs]]</code> or <code>[extract]</code> cannot go back to 3.7.0 after starting on 3.8. <strong>3.8.1</strong> and <strong>3.8.2</strong> fix document backfills that an empty file and a slow gateway held up, log and count every retried fetch, and list documents given up on at <code>/ipfs/gave-up</code>. <strong>3.8.3</strong> keys a runtime nest's cursor, health and metrics by the route it is served under, and <code>emit dune</code> translates the authored views whose DuneSQL is exact and names the construct that stopped the rest. <strong>3.8.4</strong> refuses to start a store whose data no longer matches the chain, contracts, start blocks, factories or extraction settings <code>nuthatch.toml</code> declares, and shows <code>nid</code> and <code>data_identity</code> at <code>/</code> and <code>/nest</code>. <strong>3.8.5</strong> fixes a <code>/sql</code> panic on scaled decimals too wide for the driver, and sealed velocity seeds that were silently skipped. Every 3.8 patch is a binary swap and a restart.</li>
        <li><strong>v3.7.0</strong> let a nest mirror its sealed segments to a bucket as it seals them (<a href={rfc('0052')} rel="noopener">RFC-0052</a>): <code>nuthatch publish sync</code>, <code>verify</code> and <code>status</code> by hand, or <code>--publish-target</code> on <code>dev</code> and <code>serve</code>. The mirror runs on its own thread, so a slow bucket delays the mirror and never a seal: against a bucket throttled to 1 MB/s, backfill ran at <strong>119,046 events a second without it and 118,502 with it</strong>. CI publishes to a real MinIO and requires Trino's counts and sums over the prefix to match DuckDB's. <code>nuthatch emit dune</code> wrote one DuneSQL query per event table, offline and deterministically (<a href={rfc0055} rel="noopener">RFC-0055</a>); it was removed in 4.1.0 with DuckDB's parser. The DuckDB memory walls are now operator settings that refuse rather than shrink, new seals are zstd with the writer profile named in the catalogue, and a declared query is refused when its plan could read more than 512 MiB. Alongside: an offchain file drop, the subgraph port tooling, and the x402 counter behind a Cargo feature the release binaries do not enable. The GraphQL surface shipped as a partial read surface, exact or a named refusal field by field, and is parked; it is not a drop-in replacement. Most operators swap the binary and restart. The exceptions: <code>NUTHATCH_SQL_MAX_CONCURRENCY</code> above 2 refuses to start unless <code>NUTHATCH_ANALYTICS_MEMORY_LIMIT</code> comes down (256MB at four permits), and a nest with a fully named, non-indexed tuple event parameter gets a new identity and re-indexes (#1364). Downgrading to 3.6.1 after running 3.7.0 is not safe.</li>
        <li><strong>v3.6.1</strong> was one fix, on a defect that had two production nests reporting perfect health while their sealed watermark had not moved in two days: a sparse nest with a single finalized row could hold <code>sealed_through</code> until twenty thousand had accumulated (#1199). The week before it: <strong>3.4.0</strong> stopped a sparse table fanning out into one Parquet file per seal cut (97,494 files on the Perpl nest, a median of 48 rows each) and built Robinhood Chain in; <strong>3.4.1</strong> made a query survive a seal replacing a segment under it; <strong>3.5.0</strong> added <code>--poll-interval</code> and <code>--finality-only</code>, the freshness dial that turns a tip-following nest's RPC bill down by two orders of magnitude when nobody reads it every two seconds (RFC-0040); <strong>3.5.1</strong> gave every concurrent DuckDB instance its own spill directory, which is what was segfaulting a nest at four <code>/sql</code> permits; <strong>3.6.0</strong> memoises a repeated <code>/sql</code> answer under a hash of everything it read. All drop-in; nothing changes on disk.</li>
        <li><strong>v3.1.0</strong> (a wasmtime 46.0.3 security update for RUSTSEC-2026-0268/0269, plus two production defects that only measurement found: a <code>tip_lag_blocks</code> gauge that read 0 on a lagging nest so no alert could fire, and a read-only nest reporting <code>ready:false</code>). <strong>v3.0.0</strong> (tagged, with prebuilt binaries and container images on ghcr). It makes authored incremental entities stable: a keyed relation declared in <code>entities.toml</code> is maintained while blocks arrive, rather than recomputed for every query. It is a drop-in binary swap for existing nests, with no config change, migration or re-index; adding an entity makes the next restart seed it from local sealed history. The release also fixes five defects found only by soaking real nests, adds six Prometheus series per entity, and attaches a GitHub build-provenance attestation to every artifact. <code>--seal-direct</code> now refuses an entity-bearing nest rather than serving an empty relation. MSRV is 1.95 - measured, not asserted.</li>
        <li><strong>2.2 is about degrading honestly.</strong> A page-corrupt sealed segment used to fail an entire <code>/sql</code> query; now it reduces its own table and the nest reports the damage - <code>degraded</code> and <code>degraded_tables</code> over HTTP, a warning line on the CLI, the same notice through MCP. The caveat states a fact about the nest rather than about the rows you happened to receive, so it cannot be dodged by a query that missed the gap. Blocks-only nests start, derive one row per block, and stop asking endpoints for every log on the chain; and the per-nest memory estimate was refitted against measurement after being roughly <strong>13x</strong> pessimistic, which had been refusing mounts that fit.</li>
        <li><strong>2.0 is the one release that moves data on disk</strong>, and it ships the command that does it. <code>nuthatch migrate</code> moves files and rewrites a config; it never re-indexes. Measured on a real two-nest deployment: <strong>0.259s</strong>. Tenancy moved into the runtime and the roost was retired - not renamed, removed - so there is one <code>nuthatch dev</code> and the directory decides what runs. Nest data is keyed by content address, so two tenants mounting the same nest share one dataset and it is never indexed twice. See the <a href="/docs/operate/upgrades/">upgrade notes</a>.</li>
        <li>Every prior line, for context: 0.6 brought portable nest bundles, the nest registry, zero-downtime N-1 upgrades, multichain runtimes, derive-first recipes and adaptive MCP tools; 0.7-0.8 the live mount/unmount surface and fault quarantine; 0.9 scaled mode's writer pool and the backfill work below; 1.0 the stability commitment itself. <strong>0.9.3 is a security patch</strong> - every earlier release is vulnerable to an arbitrary file read on <code>/sql</code>, because DuckDB accepts a quoted function name and the guard only matched an unquoted one. <strong>0.6.2 was also a security patch</strong>, for <code>;</code>-stacked statements, which was an arbitrary file-<em>write</em> primitive. If you expose <code>/sql</code> to anyone you do not trust, upgrade. <code>cargo install --git</code> needs <code>+1.95.0</code> exactly - <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code> does not apply to it, and rustc 1.97 hits a dbsp ICE.</li>
        <li>The Lodestar migration (<a href={rfc('0011')} rel="noopener">RFC-0011</a>): a public dashboard's live panels read from self-hosted Nuthatch nests instead of The Graph gateway, each surface checked against the gateway before its cutover, and since 2026-09-06 it holds no Graph API key and has no gateway client (<a href="/stories">the story is here</a>). The <a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/horizon-nest" rel="noopener">published <code>horizon-nest</code></a> carries the Horizon staking/service surface.</li>
        <li>A CI footprint job fails the build above 256 MB of RAM on a fixed 8,004-row fixture.</li>
        <li>A release gate serves a copy of the largest nest we run, the Lodestar allocations nest, under its production limits and runs the 74 statements its consumers send; a peak over 2 GiB fails it. 4.4.0 peaked at 1,595 to 1,788 MiB over four runs on Linux, two statements at a time as production runs them (<a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch/releases/tag/v4.4.0" rel="noopener">release notes</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- IN PROGRESS -->
      <h2 class="stat s-progress">In progress</h2>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>
          <code>nuthatch watch</code>, a read-only terminal view of a running nest: the tip, the seal, memory against its budget and the RPC endpoints (<a href={rfc('0069')} rel="noopener">RFC-0069</a>). Built on main and arriving in 5.10.0; it is not in 5.9.0.
        </li>
        <li>
          Of the reth ExEx groundwork (<a href={rfc('0003')} rel="noopener">RFC-0003</a>), the <code>Source</code> trait and the library split are in; the <code>exex</code> feature's stub was removed in 5.0.0, and the ExEx itself is designed, not built. Wiring it needs a synced node and the work is deferred; it is listed under Planned.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- PLANNED -->
      <h2 class="stat s-planned">Planned</h2>
        <p class="note">
          <strong>The 2026 feature freeze ran from 2026-08-20 to 2026-09-08, and is lifted.</strong>
          The work since began with the RFC-0044 to RFC-0048 programme (the subgraph port, offchain data,
          x402 at the counter, the lakehouse commitments and pricing query access), plus RFC-0052 and
          RFC-0053. Accepted since 2026-10-07: <a href={rfc('0062')} rel="noopener">RFC-0062</a>
          (maintained views, stopped after S2, shipped in 4.14.0), <a href={rfc('0054')} rel="noopener">RFC-0054</a>
          (the head count, 4.15.0), <a href={rfc('0063')} rel="noopener">RFC-0063</a> and
          <a href={rfc('0064')} rel="noopener">RFC-0064</a> (address history and the deposit sweep, 5.1.0 to 5.7.0),
          <a href={rfc('0065')} rel="noopener">RFC-0065</a> and <a href={rfc('0066')} rel="noopener">RFC-0066</a>
          (the tab and the nest builder, at <a href="/play">/play</a>), <a href={rfc('0068')} rel="noopener">RFC-0068</a>
          (postcards, live), <a href={rfc('0071')} rel="noopener">RFC-0071</a> (the diff, 5.9.0) and
          <a href={rfc('0069')} rel="noopener">RFC-0069</a> (the engine room, in progress).
          <a href={rfc('0070')} rel="noopener">RFC-0070</a> (time-lapse) and
          <a href={rfc('0072')} rel="noopener">RFC-0072</a> (chain weather) were parked on 2026-10-22. What the freeze deferred stays deferred, and reopens one item at a time
          (<code>docs/frozen-for-2027.md</code>). Nothing here is repudiated.
          <a href={rfc('0058')} rel="noopener">RFC-0058</a> (cross-nest SQL),
          <a href={rfc('0059')} rel="noopener">RFC-0059</a> (checkpointed folds) and
          <a href={rfc('0060')} rel="noopener">RFC-0060</a> (the Network Subgraph endpoint) were
          accepted and then parked on 2026-09-26; what was built of RFC-0059 stays behind the
          off-by-default <code>folds</code> feature.
        </p>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>
          A Homebrew tap, the one piece of <a href={rfc('0005')} rel="noopener">RFC-0005</a> still open. The releases, container images, one-line installer and build-provenance attestations (since 3.0.0) are out.
        </li>
        <li>
          reth ExEx tip mode (<a href={rfc('0003')} rel="noopener">RFC-0003</a>) - in-process
          tip-following against a colocated node, and then firehose-class extraction of traces and
          state diffs (<a href={rfc('0014')} rel="noopener">RFC-0014</a>), both gated on wiring the
          ExEx to a synced node.
        </li>
        <li>GraphQL for subgraph-shaped clients (<a href={rfc('0053')} rel="noopener">RFC-0053</a>) - shipped in 3.7.0 as a partial read surface, exact for a subgraph's event-shaped fields and a named refusal for the rest, and now parked. The default binary has no GraphQL route; since 4.11.0 every release attaches a <code>nuthatch-graph-&lt;target&gt;</code> download that serves it. An analytics client cannot adopt it unmodified, because GraphQL refuses a whole query for one unanswerable field.</li>
        <li>IVM generalisation <span class="rfc-todo">(RFC to come)</span> - lift more derived views from SQL over sealed data into incrementally-maintained views.</li>
        <li>Wildcard, signature-scoped indexing <span class="rfc-todo">(RFC to come)</span> - index an event by its signature across every emitter.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- NOT PLANNED -->
      <h2 class="stat s-none">Not planned</h2>
      <ul class="rl">
        <li>A token or decentralized-network features.</li>
        <li>Telemetry on by default, or any that identifies you. The one opt-in is the <a href="/count">head count</a>.</li>
        <li>A Kubernetes-first deployment.</li>
        <li>No gateway, auth, or metering layer in the core - those stay outside it, so independent operators can run Nuthatch behind their own.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="grp-note">
        These are design decisions, not gaps - the project's scope rules explain why.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider close-div" />
      <p class="signoff">
        The <a href={releaseNotes} rel="noopener">release notes</a>, one page per version, are the
        change-log of record, and they move faster than this page. Where this page and the notes
        disagree, trust the notes - and read the <a href={rfcsDir} rel="noopener">RFCs</a> for the reasoning behind
        each line.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
